---
import BaseLayout from '@/layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import GraphModuleClient from '@/components/shared/GraphModuleClient';

const title = 'How to Graph a Quadratic Function | EasyEquate';
const description = 'Learn how to graph a quadratic function step by step. Find the vertex, axis of symmetry, and x-intercepts with worked examples and a live graphing tool.';
const canonical = 'https://www.easyequate.com/learn/how-to-graph-a-quadratic-function';

const faqSchema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "FAQPage",
  "mainEntity": [
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "What shape does a quadratic function make?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "A quadratic function forms a U-shaped curve called a parabola. If 'a' is positive, it opens upwards. If 'a' is negative, it opens downwards."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "How do you find the vertex of a parabola?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "The x-coordinate of the vertex is found using the formula x = -b / (2a). Then, plug this x-value back into the original function to find the y-coordinate."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "What is the axis of symmetry?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "The axis of symmetry is the vertical line that divides the parabola exactly in half. It passes through the vertex and its equation is x = -b / (2a)."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "How do you find x-intercepts of a quadratic?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "You can find x-intercepts (also called roots or zeros) by setting y = 0 and solving for x. You can solve by factoring, completing the square, or using the quadratic formula."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "Does a quadratic equation ax² + bx + c = 0 always have two roots?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Yes. By the Fundamental Theorem of Algebra, every degree 2 polynomial has exactly two roots in the complex number system: either two distinct real roots (b² - 4ac > 0), one repeated real root of multiplicity 2 (b² - 4ac = 0), or two complex conjugate roots (b² - 4ac < 0)."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "How do you know if a parabola opens upwards or downwards?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Look at the leading coefficient 'a' in y = ax² + bx + c. If a > 0 (positive), the parabola opens upwards like a 'U' and the vertex is a minimum point. If a < 0 (negative), the parabola opens downwards and the vertex is a maximum point."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "How do you convert standard form to vertex form?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "You can either complete the square or find the vertex (h, k) using h = -b / (2a) and k = f(h). Then substitute these values into vertex form y = a(x - h)² + k, retaining the original leading coefficient 'a'."
      }
    }
  ]
};
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={description} canonical={canonical} jsonLd={faqSchema}>
  <article class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 py-8 prose prose-brown dark:prose-invert pt-10 sm:pt-8 sm:pl-36 md:pl-44">
    <div class="mb-6">
      <a href="/learn" class="text-sm font-medium text-[#5D4037] dark:text-[#D7CCC8] hover:underline">&larr; Back to Guides</a>
    </div>

    <h1 class="text-3xl sm:text-4xl font-bold text-[#5D4037] dark:text-[#EFEBE9] mb-4">How to Graph a Quadratic Function</h1>
    <p class="lead text-lg text-[#131313] dark:text-[#E0E0E0] mb-8">
      A visual walkthrough on sketching parabolas. Discover how to identify the vertex, axis of symmetry, intercepts, and orientation step by step.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-[#5D4037] dark:text-[#EFEBE9] mt-8 mb-4">1. Key Features of a Parabola</h2>
    <p>
      The standard form of a quadratic function is:
    </p>
    <div class="bg-[#EFEBE9] dark:bg-[#3E2723] p-4 rounded-lg my-4 text-center font-mono text-lg">
      f(x) = ax² + bx + c
    </div>
    <p>
      Every quadratic function produces a symmetric curve called a <strong>parabola</strong>. Here is what each constant controls:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Sign of a:</strong> If <code>a &gt; 0</code>, the parabola opens <em>upwards</em> (curving upward with a minimum vertex). If <code>a &lt; 0</code>, it opens <em>downwards</em> (with a maximum point).</li>
      <li><strong>Vertex:</strong> The turning point of the curve.</li>
      <li><strong>Axis of Symmetry:</strong> The central vertical line running straight through the vertex.</li>
      <li><strong>y-intercept:</strong> The point where the curve crosses the y-axis, which is simply <code>(0, c)</code>.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-[#5D4037] dark:text-[#EFEBE9] mt-8 mb-4">2. The 4-Step Graphing Method</h2>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>Find the Vertex:</strong> Calculate <code>x = -b / (2a)</code>. Then compute <code>f(x)</code> to get the y-coordinate. Your vertex is <code>(x, y)</code>.</li>
      <li><strong>Draw the Axis of Symmetry:</strong> A dashed vertical line at <code>x = -b / (2a)</code>.</li>
      <li><strong>Find the y-intercept:</strong> Plug in <code>x = 0</code> to get <code>(0, c)</code>. Then use symmetry to plot the mirror point across the axis.</li>
      <li><strong>Find the x-intercepts (Roots):</strong> Set <code>ax² + bx + c = 0</code> and solve via factoring or the quadratic formula.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-[#5D4037] dark:text-[#EFEBE9] mt-8 mb-4">3. Worked Example: f(x) = x² - 4x + 3</h2>
    <div class="p-4 bg-white dark:bg-[#131313] border border-[#E0E0E0] dark:border-[#3E2723] rounded-xl not-prose space-y-2 text-sm">
      <div><strong>Step 1: Vertex:</strong> a = 1, b = -4 &rArr; x = -(-4) / (2*1) = 2. Plug in x = 2: f(2) = 4 - 8 + 3 = -1. Vertex is <code>(2, -1)</code>.</div>
      <div><strong>Step 2: Axis of symmetry:</strong> <code>x = 2</code>.</div>
      <div><strong>Step 3: y-intercept:</strong> When x = 0, y = 3. Point: <code>(0, 3)</code>. Reflected across x = 2 gives <code>(4, 3)</code>.</div>
      <div><strong>Step 4: Roots:</strong> x² - 4x + 3 = (x - 1)(x - 3) = 0 &rArr; Roots at <code>x = 1</code> and <code>x = 3</code>.</div>
    </div>

    <div class="not-prose my-8 p-6 bg-white dark:bg-[#131313] border border-[#E0E0E0] dark:border-[#3E2723] rounded-2xl shadow-sm">
      <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-[#5D4037] dark:text-[#EFEBE9] mb-2">Plot f(x) = x² - 4x + 3 Live</h3>
      <p class="text-sm text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-400 mb-4">
        Try entering <code>x^2 - 4x + 3</code> into the graph plotter below. Notice the vertex at (2, -1) and intercepts at x = 1 and 3!
      </p>
      <div class="h-[500px] border border-[#E0E0E0] dark:border-[#3E2723] rounded-xl overflow-hidden">
        <GraphModuleClient client:only="react" />
      </div>
    </div>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-[#5D4037] dark:text-[#EFEBE9] mt-12 mb-6">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
    <div class="space-y-4">
      {faqSchema.mainEntity.map((item) => (
        <div class="border border-[#E0E0E0] dark:border-[#3E2723] rounded-lg p-4 bg-white dark:bg-[#131313]">
          <h3 class="font-bold text-[#5D4037] dark:text-[#EFEBE9] mb-2 text-base">{item.name}</h3>
          <p class="text-sm text-[#131313] dark:text-[#E0E0E0] m-0">{item.acceptedAnswer.text}</p>
        </div>
      ))}
    </div>

    <div class="mt-12 pt-6 border-t border-[#E0E0E0] dark:border-[#3E2723]">
      <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-[#5D4037] dark:text-[#EFEBE9] mb-4">More Resources:</h3>
      <div class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-4 not-prose">
        <a href="/quadratic-equation-solver" class="p-4 border border-[#E0E0E0] dark:border-[#3E2723] rounded-xl hover:shadow-md transition-shadow bg-white dark:bg-[#131313]">
          <span class="text-xs text-[#5D4037] dark:text-[#D7CCC8] font-bold block mb-1">INTERACTIVE SOLVER &rarr;</span>
          <span class="font-bold text-[#131313] dark:text-white">Quadratic Equation Solver with Steps</span>
        </a>
        <a href="/learn" class="p-4 border border-[#E0E0E0] dark:border-[#3E2723] rounded-xl hover:shadow-md transition-shadow bg-white dark:bg-[#131313]">
          <span class="text-xs text-[#5D4037] dark:text-[#D7CCC8] font-bold block mb-1">ALL GUIDES &rarr;</span>
          <span class="font-bold text-[#131313] dark:text-white">Math Learning Hub</span>
        </a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
